# =============================================================================
# Trivy Ignore File
# =============================================================================
# CVEs listed here are suppressed from Trivy vulnerability scans.
# Each entry must be justified. Review quarterly.
#
# Last reviewed: 2026-08-28   (previous review: 2026-02-23)
#
# ── How this review was done — repeat these steps next quarter ───────────────
# 1. Read .github/workflows/security-scan.yml and confirm what Trivy actually
#    scans. As of 2026-08-28 there are two scans, both BLOCKING:
#      (a) `fs` scan of the whole checkout, severity CRITICAL,HIGH. It runs
#          AFTER `composer install` — so vendor/ IS scanned — but BEFORE
#          `npm ci`, so npm findings come from the COMMITTED LOCKFILES, not
#          from an installed node_modules tree.
#      (b) image scan of the Dockerfile build, CRITICAL,HIGH, push-only, with
#          `ignore-unfixed: true` — so a CVE Debian has not fixed cannot fire
#          at all, whatever this file says.
# 2. For each entry, resolve CVE -> package + first patched version from the
#    GitHub Advisory Database (also Trivy's source for npm and pip advisories):
#      gh api "/advisories?cve_id=CVE-2026-XXXXX"
#      gh api advisories/GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx      (no leading slash on Windows)
# 3. Compare that fixed version against what is COMMITTED in every manifest the
#    fs scan can see: composer.lock, package-lock.json, react-frontend/,
#    web-uk/, mobile/, e2e/ and aspnet-backend/e2e/package-lock.json. Check
#    `git status` on those files first — an uncommitted lockfile is not the
#    thing CI scans.
# 4. Delete every entry whose package is gone, or is at/above the fixed
#    version. Keep only what is still reachable AND still unfixed, and say why.
#
# 2026-08-28 outcome: 22 of 67 entries deleted as provably stale — every one of
# them was a package that had since been upgraded past its fix (xmldom, yaml,
# picomatch, brace-expansion, node-forge, undici, ws, vite). 19 entries kept
# with re-verified justifications. 26 linux-libc-dev kernel entries kept but
# UNRESOLVED — see the note in that block; they need one experiment to settle.
# =============================================================================

# ── linux-libc-dev kernel CVEs (NOT exploitable in Docker containers) ───────
# These are all Linux kernel vulnerabilities found in the Debian base image
# (php:8.2.30-apache). Docker containers share the HOST kernel — the
# linux-libc-dev package only provides kernel header files for compilation, NOT
# the running kernel. These CVEs require:
#   - Direct access to host kernel (not available in containers)
#   - Specific hardware (GPU, NIC, PCI devices) not present in containers
#   - Ability to mount arbitrary filesystems (blocked in containers)
#   - Kernel module loading privileges (blocked in containers)
# The container runs as an unprivileged Apache process with no kernel access.
# Only the container scan can ever raise these; the fs scan does not read OS
# packages. That scan also sets `ignore-unfixed: true`.
#
# 🔴 2026-08-28 review — the version facts below were out of date and are now
# corrected, but the entries could NOT be resolved either way from this machine.
# The old note read "Fix available: linux-libc-dev 6.12.69-1 (installed:
# 6.12.63-1)". Measured today inside nexus-php-app (built from this repo's
# Dockerfile, image built 2026-08-04):
#     dpkg-query -W linux-libc-dev  ->  6.12.100-1
# i.e. the installed headers are already far past the 6.12.69-1 that the 26
# entries below were suppressed for, because the Dockerfile runs `apt-get -y
# upgrade` on every build. That strongly suggests those 26 are now dead weight.
# It is NOT proof: confirming it needs Debian's security tracker per CVE, which
# was not reachable during this review, and a wrong deletion turns the blocking
# container gate red on the next push to main.
#
# To settle it: delete the 26 "fix in 6.12.69-1" entries below in their own
# commit, push, and watch the `Container Security Scan` job (it runs on push).
# If it stays green they were stale; if it reds, restore exactly the CVEs it
# names. The five "ancient" entries above them are separate — Debian never
# fixed those, so `ignore-unfixed: true` already skips them and they are
# harmless either way.

# Ancient disputed CVEs (no fix, won't-fix) — kept 2026-08-28.
# Already covered by `ignore-unfixed: true`; retained as belt-and-braces.
CVE-2013-7445
CVE-2019-19449
CVE-2019-19814
CVE-2021-3847
CVE-2021-3864

