# ── vendor/mockery/mockery/docs/requirements.txt ────────────────────────────
# These CVEs are in a Python requirements.txt file inside the docs/ directory
# of the Mockery PHP test-only library (vendor/mockery/mockery). This file:
#   - Is documentation for the Mockery project itself, not our code
#   - Contains Python dependencies we never install or run (our stack has no Python)
#   - Lives in vendor/ which is NEVER deployed to production (gitignored)
#   - Has zero exploitability in our context (wrong language, wrong environment)
# These are false positives for our threat model. The mockery/mockery package
# is a dev-only dependency used only in PHPUnit tests.
#
# 2026-08-28 review — ALL STILL VALID, and re-verified rather than assumed:
#   - composer.lock has mockery/mockery 1.6.12 in `packages-dev` only, never in
#     `packages`, so it is genuinely test-only.
#   - The file really does reach vendor/ in CI: mockery's .gitattributes at tag
#     1.6.12 does not export-ignore /docs, so the composer dist zipball carries
#     docs/requirements.txt. This is not a phantom suppression.
#   - Every pinned version in that file is still inside its vulnerable range:
#     setuptools==69.2.0, urllib3==2.2.1, requests==2.31.0, wheel==0.43.0,
#     Pygments==2.17.2. Nothing here has aged out; removing any entry would
#     red the blocking fs scan.
# The only way to retire this block is a mockery release that drops or repins
# docs/requirements.txt. Re-check the pinned versions above next quarter.
CVE-2024-6345
CVE-2024-37891
CVE-2025-47273
CVE-2025-50181
CVE-2025-50182
CVE-2025-66418
CVE-2025-66471
CVE-2026-4539
CVE-2026-21441
CVE-2026-24049
